# A-10, L §5.18 Explanation order: "A min, max, argmin or argmax is explained by its first winning term in binding
# order" (E §10's table: "explained as that term is"). max(a, b) = 7 is b's row alone (e1); min over c is 2, won by
# c[q] and c[r], and binding order over an open index is the text order of stable ids (L §2.5), so c[q] (e2); argmax
# gives p, explained by c[p] (e3).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
index ix: open
input a: int
input b: int
input c[k in ix]: int
def y: int = max(a, b) at reader
def z: int = min(c[k] for k in ix) at reader
def w: key ix = argmax(c[k] for k in ix) at reader

version v
  cite s
  governs 2026..
  known 2026-01-01
  a = 3 "row a"
  b = 7 "row b"
  c[p] = 5 "row cp"
  c[q] = 2 "row cq"
  c[r] = 2 "row cr"

ask e1: explain y law 2026-06-30
ask e2: explain z law 2026-06-30
ask e3: explain w law 2026-06-30
=== expect
e1: 7
  [1] y = 7, law date 2026-06-30
      rule base: won
        [2] row v:b = 7
            version v, source s, "s", locator "row b", governs 2026-01-01.., known 2026-01-01
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-12-31
e2: 2
  [1] z = 2, law date 2026-06-30
      rule base: won
        [2] row v:c[q] = 2
            version v, source s, "s", locator "row cq", governs 2026-01-01.., known 2026-01-01
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-12-31
e3: p
  [1] w = p, law date 2026-06-30
      rule base: won
        [2] row v:c[p] = 5
            version v, source s, "s", locator "row cp", governs 2026-01-01.., known 2026-01-01
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-12-31
